['You (New Joiner)', q{Then I will keep doing it. <strong>Left column: what I heard. Right column: what I think it means.</strong> After the meeting, the right column gets checked — <em>yours being the preferred authority, Marco.</em>}, q{那我就继续。<strong>左栏：我听到的。右栏：我以为的意思。</strong>会后，右栏会被验证——<em>Marco，首选权威就是你</em>。}],
['Priya (Delivery)', q{Since we are decoding — <em>new joiner survival list.</strong> "BCP" is business continuity plan — <em>"we will BCP it" means there is a fallback process on paper.</em> A "chaser" is a follow-up message. And <strong>"the business" means whoever asked for the thing, by seniority</strong>.}, q{既然在破译——<em>新人生存清单。</strong>"BCP" 是业务连续性计划——<em>"we will BCP it" 意思是纸面上有兜底流程。</em>"chaser" 是跟进消息。<strong>"the business" 指提出需求的那方，按资历排。</strong>}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{Saving all three. <strong>Last decoder question:</strong> when someone says <em>"that needs a spacer"</em> — is that a calendar thing or a data thing? I heard it twice today and got two different vibes.}, q{三个都记下。<strong>最后一个破译问题：</strong>有人 <em>"that needs a spacer"</em>——是日历的事还是数据的事？今天听到两次，两次的语境完全不同。}],
['Elena (Tech Lead)', q{Data thing — <strong>a padding field</strong> in the extract. But your instinct to ask instead of guess is exactly right: <em>half our acronyms mean two things in two teams</em>. When in doubt, <strong>ask which team\'s spacer it is.</strong>}, q{数据的事——抽取文件里的<strong>填充字段</strong>。但你"问而不猜"的直觉完全正确：<em>我们一半的缩写在两个团队里是两个意思</em>。拿不准时，<strong>问清是哪个团队的 spacer。</strong>}],
],
