grammar => [
['Past simple inventory for "yesterday"', q{<em>"I read the doc, got access live, and sat with Daniel B."</em> — three past-simple verbs, no connective tissue. The standup yesterday-line is an inventory, not a narrative: verbs in series, each one a completed item. If you need "and then", you are telling a story — trim it back to the list.}, q{"I read the doc, got access live, and sat with Daniel B."——三个一般过去时动词，无连接组织。站会的"昨天"是清单不是叙事：动词并列，每项已完成。如果你需要"and then"，你就在讲故事——砍回到清单。}],
['The "not X — Y-ing" correction frame', q{<em>"Not stuck — flagging the dependency."</em>, <em>"not a complaint — a fact."</em> The <strong>not + adjective, -ing + noun</strong> frame re-labels your own message before anyone else does it. It is the politest way to say "read this correctly": deny the loud interpretation, name the precise one. New joiners who master this frame sound senior in week one.}, q{"Not stuck — flagging the dependency."、"not a complaint — a fact."——<strong>not + 形容词, -ing + 名词</strong>句式在别人误读之前先给自己的信息重新贴标签。这是"请正确理解我"最礼貌的说法：否认响亮的解读，命名精确的那个。掌握这个句式的新人，第一周听起来就很资深。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Week-one standups report orientation, not output.</strong> "Three systems now make sense" is a legitimate yesterday.},
q{<strong>Keep it to 45 seconds.</strong> Three lines. If your week-one update runs two minutes, you are narrating, not reporting.},
q{<strong>Track your own tickets out loud.</strong> "Day two of SLA" tells the team you run your own follow-ups — trust arrives early for people who count days.},
q{<strong>Soft blockers stay soft until they are hard.</strong> Flag the dependency, state the fallback ("I will pair rather than wait"), and only then does it graduate to a blocker.},
q{<strong>Answer "what was hardest" with a patch.</strong> The doc-fix answer made you a contributor on day three. Look for one per week.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Your first three lines', p => 'Write tomorrow\'s standup update as a new joiner: yesterday (3 items), today (1 goal + why), blocker (status + fallback). 45 seconds when spoken.', a => q{Read it aloud with a timer. If it passes 60 seconds, cut adjectives, keep facts. The delta sentence ("X now makes sense that did not yesterday") is worth keeping even under time pressure — it is your week-one currency.} },
{ t => 'Blocker or dependency?', p => 'Classify: (a) access ticket day 2 of 2-day SLA; (b) access ticket day 5, no reply; (c) waiting on a colleague\'s review to start your task.', a => q{(a) dependency — flag only. (b) blocker — bump in the ticket, mention in standup. (c) dependency — with a named fallback (start with test data, pair). The classification decides the tone: dependencies get patience, blockers get motion.} },
{ t => 'The doc question', p => 'Elena asks which section was hardest. Write your answer using the three-part shape: which section, what it assumed, what one line would fix.', a => q{Model: "The GLM section — it assumed ledger-mapping knowledge. A one-line definition at the top would save every future joiner twenty minutes." Specific + generous: you are improving the doc for the next person, which is the cheapest reputation a new joiner can buy.} },
],
},
