grammar => [
['Either/or inside the gap', q{<em>"Is idempotency the job\'s responsibility or the framework\'s?"</em> — the <strong>X or Y</strong> question form forces a decision-sized answer. Compare the open version: "How does idempotency work here?" (a lecture request). The either/or gap says: I understand both candidates; I need the ruling. Experts answer these in one sentence — and feel smart doing it, which is why they lean in.}, q{"Is idempotency the job\'s responsibility or the framework\'s?"——<strong>X or Y</strong> 问句逼出决策尺寸的答案。对比开放式："How does idempotency work here?"（索要一场讲座）。either/or 缺口表明：两个候选我都懂；我要的是裁决。专家一句话就能回答——而且答得很有成就感，所以他们身体前倾。}],
['The dash as a loop-close', q{<em>"Whatever the answer is, I will add it to the onboarding doc."</em> — <strong>whatever X, I will Y</strong> commits to the loop before knowing the answer. It works because it is unconditional: the doc entry happens either way, so helping you costs the expert one answer and earns them a wiki page. Grammar of reciprocity.}, q{"Whatever the answer is, I will add it to the onboarding doc."——<strong>whatever X, I will Y</strong> 在知道答案之前就承诺闭环。它有效是因为无条件：无论结果如何，文档条目都会发生——帮你一次，专家赚回一页 wiki。这是互惠的语法。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Four parts, in order.</strong> Context, attempt, gap, loop-close. Skipping the attempt is what makes questions feel silly.},
q{<strong>The failure is the credential.</strong> "I tried the naive join and got duplicates" buys you twelve minutes of whiteboard; "how does this work?" buys you a link.},
q{<strong>Size the gap to one decision.</strong> If the answer needs a chapter, you have two or three questions — find the first fork.},
q{<strong>Offer the loop-close.</strong> Doc entries, test cases, wiki updates — the answer should land somewhere permanent. That is why people invest in your questions.},
q{<strong>Watch the good askers.</strong> Daniel K\'s questions have a shape because he built it deliberately. Steal the shape, not the questions.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Rebuild a question', p => 'Take a question you asked this week (or wanted to). Rebuild it in the four-part frame: context, attempt, gap (decision-sized), loop-close.', a => q{If you cannot fill "attempt", go do the naive version first — the question will improve by itself. If the gap fills a chapter, split it and pick the first fork. The frame is also a filter: some questions dissolve under their own homework.} },
{ t => 'Gap sizing', p => 'Shrink this chapter-sized gap into one either/or decision: "I do not understand how our deployment pipeline works."', a => q{Model: "I am trying to ship my first fix. I pushed to the feature branch and CI ran green. My gap: does merging to main auto-deploy to UAT, or is there an approval gate I should request first?" Context, attempt, one fork — answerable in one sentence by anyone on the team.} },
{ t => 'The loop-close menu', p => 'List three loop-closes you can realistically offer in your first month: doc entries, test cases, a diagram. Match each to the type of question it fits.', a => q{Doc entry fits definitional questions ("what does X mean here"); test case fits behaviour questions ("what happens when X fails"); diagram fits topology questions ("what feeds what"). Matching the artifact to the question type is what makes the offer credible.} },
],
},
