['Daniel B (Senior Dev)', q{<strong>Me first — I have been through compliance four times</strong> and know which form actually moves. If I am dark, Priya escalates; she owns the unblocking muscle. <em>Elena only hears about it if compliance says no</em> — she is the architecture decision, not the process nudge.}, q{<strong>先找我——合规这条路我走过四次</strong>，知道哪张表真的管用。如果我不在，Priya 负责升级；解阻塞的肌肉归她。<em>Elena 只在合规说不时才出场</em>——她管架构决策，不管流程推动。}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{That is exactly the map — <strong>three names, three different jobs, one question.</strong> Last routing test: <em>who must not be surprised?</em> I hear that phrase in every onboarding doc and nobody defines it.}, q{这正是我要的地图——<strong>三个名字、三种分工、一个问题。</strong>最后一个路由测试：<em>谁不能被惊吓？</em>每份入职文档里都有这句话，但没人定义它。}],
['Daniel B (Senior Dev)', q{<strong>Whoever owns the thing before your work touches it.</strong> Your dashboard reads my pipeline? <em>I should hear about scope changes before your demo does.</em> It is not permission — it is <strong>no-surprise courtesy</strong>, and the phrase means: <em>if learning it from the meeting would feel like an ambush, that is who.</em>}, q{<strong>在你的工作触及之前就拥有那件事的人。</strong>你的仪表盘读我的管道？<em>范围变更应该在你的演示之前让我知道。</em>这不是审批权——这是<strong>"不惊吓"的礼节</strong>，这句话的意思是：<em>如果从会议上得知会像被伏击，那个人就是。</em>}],
