grammar => [
['The three-part message as parallel syntax', q{<em>"Context: I am building X. Attempts: I tried Y, got Z. Ask: fifteen minutes to choose between A and B."</em> — label-first syntax. Each part opens with its label, so the reader can stop reading the moment their answer lives. Labels convert a message from prose into a form — and forms get answered.}, q{"Context: I am building X. Attempts: I tried Y, got Z. Ask: fifteen minutes to choose between A and B."——标签先行句法。每部分以标签开头，读者在答案所在处即可停止阅读。标签把消息从散文变成表格——而表格会被回复。}],
['"Neither, actually" — the ruling sentence', q{<em>"Answer: neither, actually — the flag is unreliable because of a known race condition."</em> — <strong>neither, actually</strong> rejects both offered options and immediately supplies the real one. The word <strong>actually</strong> softens the reversal: your binary was reasonable; the world just had a third option. Experts use this frame constantly when juniors bring well-formed but incomplete questions.}, q{"Answer: neither, actually — the flag is unreliable because of a known race condition."——<strong>neither, actually</strong> 同时否掉两个候选并立刻给出真答案。<strong>actually</strong> 缓和了反转：你的二选一是合理的，只是世界还有第三个选项。当新人带来格式良好但不完整的问题时，专家总是用这个句式。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>The 15-minute rule has a quieter twin: the visible-struggle rule.</strong> Struggle is fine; invisible struggle is not. Counting minutes out loud fixes both.},
q{<strong>Never send a bare error message.</strong> "This query fails, any ideas?" costs the helper ten minutes of archaeology. Your context is their shortcut.},
q{<strong>Ask in writing, offer to come over.</strong> The message respects their calendar; the offer respects the option of a fast whiteboard.},
q{<strong>Close the loop in the ticket.</strong> Your findings are the next person\'s head start — the best askers become maintainers of the team\'s collective memory.},
q{<strong>"One layer away" is a win.</strong> It means you dug to the correct depth and only lacked the map. Say it with a laugh, then write it down.},
],
